WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.210 --> 00:00:03.090
This shot is a rough version

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:05.700
of the final battle in
"Avengers: Endgame."

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.690
And it looks pretty similar
to what you saw in theaters.

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:11.010
But the process of putting
this rough shot together

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:14.070
began three years before
the movie came out --

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:15.920
well before the actors went on set

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.400
and before a single frame
was captured on camera.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.950
These rough shots are called previs,

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:24.020
short for previsualization.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:26.230
Essentially, this tool allows filmmakers

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:29.550
to digitally map out how they
want any given scene to look.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.570
Directors can then use the previs

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:33.700
as a guide for how to film scenes,

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:37.303
sometimes frame for frame,
once everyone's on set.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.690
Previs has been around for a long time.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:42.820
But in earlier movies,

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:45.370
it was used in a much more limited way.

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:47.112
Chris Edwards: They were used for just

00:00:47.112 --> 00:00:49.345
small pockets of that movie.

00:00:49.345 --> 00:00:51.450
For the one big visual effect shot,

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:53.420
for the big finale sequence.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.410
Narrator: But now, instead
of just using previs

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:58.010
in small doses, companies
like The Third Floor

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:02.020
have developed it into a tool
for designing entire movies.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:05.290
And no studio's embraced that
approach more than Marvel,

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:07.150
which has enlisted the
help of The Third Floor

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:11.320
for 19 of the 23 films
in its Infinity Saga.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:14.160
For 2014's "Captain America:
The Winter Soldier,"

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:15.410
Marvel had gotten to the point

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:18.020
of previsualizing two-thirds of a movie.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.640
Today, the studio is
known to use visualization

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.860
to map out every single scene.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:24.540
Not just action scenes, either;

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:27.220
for "Endgame," The Third
Floor's work touched everything

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:29.590
from the comedic scene
with Hulk in the diner

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:32.210
to emotional moments like
the one between Hawkeye

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:33.826
and Black Widow on Vormir,

00:01:33.826 --> 00:01:35.410
leaving nothing up to chance

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:38.153
in one of the most
expensive movies ever made.

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:42.120
The first visualizations of a Marvel film

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.739
will often begin before the
script's fully finished.

00:01:44.739 --> 00:01:46.300
Chris: And that actually is the best place

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.460
for a professional visualization team,

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:52.400
is to be able to make bridges
and try out some experiments

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.440
between departments that have a crazy idea

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.100
that may or may not work.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:59.290
You want to be able to
throw things at walls

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:00.670
and see if they stick.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:03.110
Narrator: Occasionally, rough
previs might be underway

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:04.940
even before the cinematographer

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:07.280
or director has signed on to the project,

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.840
a practice that can be
controversial in Hollywood.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.910
Director Lucrecia Martel said
she turned down the offer

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:14.890
to direct the upcoming "Black Widow" movie

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:16.820
after Marvel told her not to worry

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:19.100
about the action sequences.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:21.390
It is true that Marvel
encourages their team

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:23.500
of previs artists to play an active role

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:25.120
in story development.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.330
Jeff Ford, the editor for "Endgame,"

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:29.800
said that The Third Floor's
visualization supervisor

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:33.150
Gerardo Ramirez, who'd worked
on nine prior Marvel films,

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:34.950
was like an additional director, writer,

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:36.750
and editor on the project.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:38.830
That's because previs artists are involved

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:41.070
in just about everything from the start,

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:43.430
helping to map out story beats and shots

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:46.470
and consulting on everything
from blocking, staging,

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:49.700
lighting, how heroes will use
their power in big fights,

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:53.300
and even the timing and
tone of any given scene.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.390
To understand just how crucial previs is

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:56.570
to Marvel's process,

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:58.980
let's go back to that final
battle from "Endgame,"

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:01.780
specifically the "Avengers
assemble" moment,

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:04.930
when all the heroes return
one by one through portals.

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:06.400
The Third Floor presented Marvel

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:09.150
with two previs'd takes
on that arrival moment.

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:11.530
One was slower and more emotional,

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:13.120
while the other was higher energy

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.330
and had the heroes arriving mid-charge.

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:19.040
Ultimately, Marvel went with
a blend of the two versions.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:20.950
A lot of this is done to save money.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:22.980
Today's previs tools allow filmmakers

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:24.860
to do virtual location scouting

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:27.400
and test out advanced camera work.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.437
Take, for example, the Sony/Marvel movie

00:03:29.437 --> 00:03:32.383
"The Amazing Spider-Man,"
which was shot in 3D.

00:03:32.383 --> 00:03:35.710
The filmmakers wanted to have
some cool first-person shots

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:37.890
of Spider-Man swinging through New York,

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:40.340
but they didn't want to
make audiences nauseous.

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:43.440
So they used previs from the
visualization studio Proof

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.820
to determine how much
and what type of movement

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:47.510
people would be able to handle.

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:49.930
All of this is then
extracted into a blueprint

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:51.570
for production called techvis,

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:54.210
which provides filmmakers
with all the data and diagrams

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:57.330
they'll need to actually
recreate each shot in real life.

00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:00.190
Essentially, this is where
all the detailed math happens.

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:02.190
Remember this scene from "Thor: Ragnarok,"

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:04.920
where Heimdall leads the
Asgardians across the bridge?

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:07.750
The Third Floor did the
techvis for that crane shot

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:10.290
to work out the camera
height and lenses they'd need

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:11.830
along with the distance from which

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:13.290
they'd film the characters.

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:16.210
For "Endgame," they
produced over 100 different

00:04:16.210 --> 00:04:18.930
technical mock-ups containing
information like this,

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:20.810
all of it accurate to the real locations

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:22.310
and equipment they'd be using.

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:23.650
Getting all the numbers down pat

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:26.150
is especially important
for a movie like "Ant-Man,"

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:28.570
where characters dramatically
fluctuate in size

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:30.010
from one moment to the next,

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:32.640
or "Doctor Strange," where
the folding-city imagery

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.300
required a lot of dimensional mathematics.

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:38.150
And techvis is useful
for quieter beats, too.

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:40.340
Marvel used it to define
the camera parameters

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:42.330
for this weighty scene between Hawkeye

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:43.920
and Black Widow in "Endgame"

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.710
to make sure they'd hit the
right tone and visual impact.

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:49.120
Filmmakers can even plug
the data from techvis

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:51.270
into a motion-control-camera system,

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:54.640
which lets you program precise,
repeatable camera movements.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:57.808
Chris: They're also well
versed in programming

00:04:57.808 --> 00:05:01.108
robotic dollies, robotic cranes

00:05:01.108 --> 00:05:03.483
that move the camera around.

00:05:03.483 --> 00:05:05.160
Narrator: Setting up
such a tightly controlled

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:07.550
shooting environment can
speed up production a lot.

00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:09.950
And when you're on location
for a Marvel movie,

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:11.123
every minute counts.

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:13.916
The visualization process

00:05:13.916 --> 00:05:15.399
doesn't end with shooting, though.

00:05:15.399 --> 00:05:17.140
The Third Floor's work on a Marvel film

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:19.620
can run all the way up to
the end of postproduction

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:21.800
because of the process called postvis.

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:23.900
This involves taking a
live-action photography

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:26.650
they've shot and combining
it with the previs elements.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.860
Take this moment in "Endgame,"
where Strange, Spider-Man,

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:33.630
and Star-Lord join the final battle.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:36.000
The Third Floor started
with completely digital

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.140
previs and techvis for the shot,

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:41.470
which Marvel then used to film
the actors on a green screen.

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:44.820
Next, The Third Floor reintroduced
some key previs elements,

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:47.550
like the background and
the outline of the portal.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.710
For "Endgame," The Third
Floor's artists made

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:52.350
7,300 previs shots.

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:55.680
Over 7,000 of those ultimately
became postvis shots.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:57.940
The postvis shots serve as placeholders

00:05:57.940 --> 00:05:59.300
in early cuts of the film,

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:01.240
allowing Marvel to quickly preview it

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:03.625
before commissioning
the finishing effects.

00:06:03.625 --> 00:06:05.800
Marvel will often screen
these versions of the film,

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:08.670
filled with postvis, in
front of test audiences

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:11.200
to get an idea of how the
film might be received.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:13.760
Plus, the postvis shots provide a template

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:15.870
to the VFX artists who take on the movie's

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:17.350
final effects work.

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:19.500
Using visualization from beginning to end

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:23.400
can save Marvel millions on
expensive effects and shoots.

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:26.260
And, ultimately, having all the
technical stuff squared away

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:28.550
will ideally free up
the director to focus on

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:31.380
getting the best performances
from their actors on set.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:33.673
Chris: They can be warned
about things that are

00:06:33.673 --> 00:06:37.060
up and coming that might
impact their craft,

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:39.130
so that they're not surprised about it

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:40.840
when they walk onto the set.

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:42.350
Narrator: But doesn't
having all of these shots

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:44.890
pre-rendered diminish
the role of directors

00:06:44.890 --> 00:06:46.230
on Marvel movies?

00:06:46.230 --> 00:06:47.910
Not necessarily.

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:50.300
Another advantage of
in-depth visualization

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:53.890
is that Marvel isn't limited
to hiring big action directors.

00:06:53.890 --> 00:06:55.150
The studio's become known

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:57.190
for their unconventional director choices,

00:06:57.190 --> 00:07:00.290
often pulling talent from
the indie or TV circuit.

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:02.560
Chris: And that's where
they can really benefit

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:04.860
also from the previs process,

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:07.800
as they can trust a team like ours

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:10.110
to work with the stunt coordinator,

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:11.310
so that all they have to do is

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:14.330
be like an avid viewer of their own film

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:17.140
as it's coming together
and to be making comments,

00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:18.630
like they do with everything else.

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:20.120
Narrator: Some directors might chafe

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:22.270
at Marvel's wide-ranging use of previs.

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:24.240
Others may welcome the
fact that Marvel has

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:27.500
a dedicated team of previs
artists and VFX specialists

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:30.145
who know how to plan out action sequences.

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:34.110
Marvel's extensive use of visualization

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:36.930
has helped the franchise
maintain a coherent vision

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:38.330
all the way through "Endgame."

00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:40.820
Given Marvel's box-office dominance,

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:42.670
other studios may follow their model

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:44.378
of embracing visualization,

00:07:44.378 --> 00:07:45.986
even for smaller productions.

00:07:45.986 --> 00:07:47.912
The Third Floor has worked on a number

00:07:47.912 --> 00:07:50.060
of nonaction films in recent years,

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:53.260
from art flicks like
2016's "Nocturnal Animals"

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:56.330
to political biopics like 2018's "Vice."

00:07:56.330 --> 00:07:59.630
Chris: Now that the
digital world is merging in

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:03.960
with the physical world, that
is going to be the future

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:07.760
of a lot larger swaths of content creation

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:12.118
than just these very
ambitious productions.

